WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.620
Today we’re back with another round of your
questions!

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:13.719
We’ve got questions about microbes, questions
about how James, our Master of Microscopes

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:18.770
takes care of microbes, and questions about
how we make these videos.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:23.150
Some of these things we’ve seen come up
frequently in comments, and others are questions

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.369
sent in via Twitter, Patreon, and Instagram.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:30.560
So thank you to everyone who’s been commenting
and asking questions, we couldn’t do any

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.980
of this without your shared excitement for
all things microcosmos.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:43.000
And every one of your comments and questions
steers this channel on our journey.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.190
Question number one, we’ve been asked if
there are any species of fungi that live in

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:49.420
the aqueous environment?

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.540
And yes, there are!

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:58.280
We featured some in our recent episode about
nematode-trapping fungi, which are very fascinating

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.740
and also a teensy bit creepy.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.290
There are also other fungal parasites.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:09.510
These amoeba are infected with Amoebophilus
simplex, a fungus that attacks certain amoeba

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.440
and then grow spores out of them.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.030
These euglenids are also infected with some
kind of fungus though we couldn’t tell you

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.240
which kind.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.170
Moving out of the water onto the land, we’ve
got a question about whether we’ve had the

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:27.690
chance to observe motile soil microbes, and
whether we’ve been able to look at species

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.250
that come from more than 1 mile down in the
ground.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:37.350
We’ve talked a bit about soil microbes,
but the fact is that the soil has an incredible

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:42.799
diversity of life in it, and we’ve barely
scratched the surface (literally).

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:47.270
And we’d love to dig deeper so that we can
see some of the organisms that live a mile

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.369
or more underground.

00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:54.060
But we cannot do that ourselves, so James
has been trying to get ahold of some samples

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.799
from drilling companies.

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:57.960
So fingers crossed on that.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.100
Next question: Do organisms like Gastrotrich
have gut flora?

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:08.400
Well they almost certainly do have gut flora.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.720
Bacteria are basically everywhere, including
in the guts of microscopic animals.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.390
As for their role in gastrotrichs?

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.970
It could be anything.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:25.040
Our own microbiome is made up of bacteria
that do everything from help us digest food

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:31.340
to manage our immune system, and nature is
full of many incredible examples of how gut

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.850
flora and other forms of microbiome inform
the lives of animals.

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:40.900
So there’s probably something cool going
on there.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:50.569
But no scientist, as far as we know, have
yet investigated it.

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:57.860
One of the most common questions we get is
how to start your own journey to the microcosmos. 

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:03.650
The easiest thing is to get some water from
outside and look at it under a microscope,

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:09.120
but of course, picking and purchasing a microscope
can be overwhelming.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.420
Like any hobby, it’s probably best to start
off simple and see what works for you, get

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.180
some experience, and then build from there.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:24.690
Also, we might be working on some things to
help you narrow down your search in the future,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.470
but we can’t talk about it yet.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:33.740
Of course, microscopes are only one part of
the microcosmos experience—the other big

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.269
thing are the microbes that you’re gonna
look at with them.

00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:42.810
And we’ve gotten a few questions about how
to best take care of our invisible friends

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.190
for the long term.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:49.810
The key is to try to get samples that are
on the larger size.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:54.120
Having a larger sample will make any environmental
changes happen slower.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.170
It’s like the difference between leaving
a bottle of water out in the sun versus a

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:02.370
whole pool of water: the bottle will get warmer
faster.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:08.220
Now, obviously you’re not gonna be gathering
a whole pool’s worth of microbes, but in

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.950
general, it’s best to err on the side of
larger samples so that you can provide your

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.769
microbes with a steady environment.

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:19.970
In addition, you’ll also want to try and
mimic as much of their original environment

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:20.970
as possible.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:26.750
So collect leaves, sediment, and aquatic plants
from wherever you’re sampling to make your

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.400
microbes feel at home.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.000
We also get a ton of questions about how to
identify microbes.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.740
We’ve made an episode about just this very
thing, but the crux of that whole discussion

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:44.860
is that…it’s really a thing built on a
lot of internet sleuthing and practice.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:51.030
But with that said, our very own James Weiss
will be publishing a book next year called

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:56.551
The Hidden Beauty of the Microscopic World,
to be published by Penguin Random House, which

00:04:56.551 --> 00:05:02.160
will be a picture heavy book that might help
you with your identification needs.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.400
Which is very exciting!

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.610
Congratulations James!

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:12.690
Moving on to a microscope question: what is
the minimum magnification needed to see single-celled

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:13.690
organisms?

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:19.650
Well, there are actually some single-celled
organisms large enough to be seen without

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:24.720
a microscope, especially if you put them in
front of a strong light like this stentor

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.810
culture.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:30.949
When it comes to using a microscope, we’ve
been able to use a magnification as low as

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:33.350
40x to see our samples.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:37.930
Now it’s time to get into some behind the
scenes stuff.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.780
Several of you have been asking about whether
there are any microbes that we want to show

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.380
but cannot for some reason.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:45.380
And yes, definitely.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.120
There are so many incredible microbes out
there, and while we’re fortunate that our

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:48.120
accessible sources have so many unique things
for us to find, there’s still plenty more

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.120
that we want to see and that we want to show
you.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.480
Right now, we’re hoping to find a super
rare ciliate called thysonomorpha, which has

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.800
weird retractable tentacles.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:56.949
And then there’s plenty on our wishlist,
a bit more of a reach.

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:02.820
We’d love to feature archaea, which are
single-celled prokaryotes distinct from bacteria.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:08.180
But archaea are super tiny, even by microcosmos
standards, so they’re hard for us to find

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:09.180
right now.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:10.180
We’re planning to get fluorescent illumination
in the future, which will let us stain for

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.180
archaeans so we can see them better.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.789
Archaeans are famed for their ability to survive
in some of the hottest and saltiest places

00:06:14.789 --> 00:06:18.990
on the planet, making them what we call extremophiles.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:25.060
And we really, really want to be able to talk
more about extremophiles because it’s incredible

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:30.979
to learn more about how microbes are able
to survive in some of the harshest environments.

00:06:30.979 --> 00:06:34.530
Unfortunately, we don’t know how to do that
yet.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:39.889
We don’t know how to take care of them or
capture them on video, and that is on our

00:06:39.889 --> 00:06:41.060
wishlist.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:44.560
We also got a question wanting to know whether
James has discovered something that’s not

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.479
just new to him, but new to science.

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:50.830
And it turns out he has!

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:55.600
He found this ciliate a few months ago and
couldn’t identify it, so he contacted some

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:58.860
scientists working on this group of organisms.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:04.819
There are reports of similar species from
the 1930s, but so far no description matches

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:06.449
what he’s found.

00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:12.919
So James is working on trying to culture them
and maybe do some genomic analysis, and hopefully

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:16.180
he’ll get to write a paper and name the
organism.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:21.759
If he gets to do that, we might even ask you
all to pitch in some ideas!

00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:26.270
Someone else asked whether we worry about
getting sick from some of the things we’re

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:30.630
working with, which is always a good question
to ask because safety is an important thing

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:35.690
to keep track of when you’ve got hundreds
of containers full of microbes in your home.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:40.680
So James exercises caution by not handling
the containers with an open wound.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.260
He also washes his hands after working with
the microscope.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:50.300
The real challenge is making sure his cats
are staying safe because cats, you know, they’re

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.300
cats.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.759
So he’s trained them to avoid his samples
by blowing puffs of air on their face when

00:07:55.759 --> 00:07:59.580
they try to climb up on his table and his
window sills.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:03.880
These are the little bits of insight you get
when you watch the questions videos.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:09.440
On to some more nitty gritty: after we’re
done observing the organism on the slide,

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:10.639
what do we do with them?

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:13.740
Do we dispose of them or culture them?

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:19.139
We don’t really try to culture everything
but instead, we try to create little copies

00:08:19.139 --> 00:08:22.199
of the environment that the organisms came
from.

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:27.130
So when we’re done checking a slide, everything
either gets washed back into the sample container

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:29.940
or into a pond aquarium.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:36.579
But if we do want to culture them, James uses
a micropipette to select the cells one by

00:08:36.579 --> 00:08:40.189
one and transfer them onto a clean water drop.

00:08:40.189 --> 00:08:45.660
He’ll repeat this a few times to wash the
cells, and then put them into some water to

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.149
culture.

00:08:47.149 --> 00:08:51.550
We get a lot of wonderful comments from you
all about the music on this channel and you’ve

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:53.850
also asked about how it’s composed.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:58.550
The music is made by the very talented Andrew
Huang, who you can find on YouTube.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:05.250
Link in the description, and he said the following:
“The music for Microcosmos is some of the

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:12.259
most organic composing I’ve ever done, where
I generally just set out to explore a mood

00:09:12.259 --> 00:09:17.279
as suggested by the team or based on my own
viewing of the series.”

00:09:17.279 --> 00:09:19.850
I think he did a really wonderful job.

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:25.249
This question’s from Andrew Taylor who asked
on YouTube: “What motivated you to create

00:09:25.249 --> 00:09:26.529
this channel?

00:09:26.529 --> 00:09:33.129
Why did you branch out into the more relaxing
narrative instead of continuing the high energy

00:09:33.129 --> 00:09:35.160
of your other productions?

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:38.720
And hey, this is actually a question for me,
neat!

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.509
Well, at first I was motivated to make the
channel after seeing some of James’ videos

00:09:43.509 --> 00:09:48.699
on YouTube and I reached out and I was like,
“We should talk about these things.”

00:09:48.699 --> 00:09:54.199
But it terms of the vibe, like when you’re
making something, what it’s gonna feel like,

00:09:54.199 --> 00:09:59.579
how it’s going to be experienced, that can’t
just come down to like what you know how to

00:09:59.579 --> 00:10:05.810
do or what you’re good at, or what’s become
expected of you, or even a particular platform

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.379
like YouTube.

00:10:07.379 --> 00:10:10.790
It has to be about what is right for the content.

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:16.949
And when I first saw James’ videos, there
was a vibe already there, and so we just sorta

00:10:16.949 --> 00:10:18.679
followed that vibe.

00:10:18.679 --> 00:10:24.779
It was also a moment when I, personally, needed
some opportunities maybe to slow down a little

00:10:24.779 --> 00:10:26.440
bit sometimes.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:31.009
That has only felt more necessary to me in
the years since we launched.

00:10:31.009 --> 00:10:36.269
So, I was excited to make something a little
more chill.

00:10:36.269 --> 00:10:41.119
And finally, from time to time we receive
comments asking us about money.

00:10:41.119 --> 00:10:46.059
Maybe asking about our Patreon, our sponsorships,
or YouTube ad revenue, but the questions mostly

00:10:46.059 --> 00:10:54.220
boil down to a curiosity about how the finances
of a YouTube channel work, and I love talking

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.110
about that stuff.

00:10:56.110 --> 00:11:02.089
Journey to the Microcosmos is produced by
a larger company, Complexly, that makes SciShow

00:11:02.089 --> 00:11:06.519
and Crash Course and Eons, and about a dozen
other YouTube channels.

00:11:06.519 --> 00:11:11.259
And when we launch something new, at that
company, we invest money that we made from

00:11:11.259 --> 00:11:16.360
other things to try something in the hopes
that the new thing will become self-sustaining.

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:18.389
It will gain an audience.

00:11:18.389 --> 00:11:19.949
It will gain support.

00:11:19.949 --> 00:11:22.980
And it will be able to continue to exist.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:29.050
When those things aren’t able to sustain
their costs, that’s when a show gets cancelled.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:33.339
We can’t make shows that cost more money
to produce than they make.

00:11:33.339 --> 00:11:35.290
So what do we spend money on?

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:36.489
Several things.

00:11:36.489 --> 00:11:41.899
Our writer, Deboki, on Matt’s editing and
graphics and production, on Andrew’s music,

00:11:41.899 --> 00:11:46.730
and, of course, helping James continue his
amazing work.

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:52.110
The one thing about this that is free is the
narration, which I guess I throw in for free

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:56.639
cuz I’m the CEO of Complexly and I just
really like to work on this show.

00:11:56.639 --> 00:12:01.689
The good news is, that because this channel
has the support of our Patreon patrons and

00:12:01.689 --> 00:12:08.249
advertisers, it’s now making enough money
that we can think not just about how to survive,

00:12:08.249 --> 00:12:11.689
but also about how to keep making our content
better.

00:12:11.689 --> 00:12:17.569
Now, a lot of these things are expensive,
but particularly with microscopy, the smallest

00:12:17.569 --> 00:12:22.910
microscope parts can cost thousands of dollars,
but thanks to your support, we’ve been able

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:29.129
to upgrade to a 4K camera to produce even
better looking footage, and recently, James

00:12:29.129 --> 00:12:36.109
able to get a brand new microscope so that
we can share the amazingness that is DIC microscopy.

00:12:36.109 --> 00:12:42.279
Our next goal is to hit $5,000 a month on
Patreon, and that’s gonna let us buy a 40x

00:12:42.279 --> 00:12:46.970
objective for the new microscope similar to
the one we’ve used in our past brightfield

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:48.889
and dark field clips.

00:12:48.889 --> 00:12:54.509
That way we can still get the stunning footage
we get with DIC microscopy, but also get a

00:12:54.509 --> 00:12:58.059
more zoomed out view of the microcosmos.

00:12:58.059 --> 00:13:02.429
So, if you’re interested in joining our
amazing group of patrons, you can head over

00:13:02.429 --> 00:13:10.220
to patreon.com/journeytomicro, and together,
we can change our perspective on the world.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:15.649
And if you can’t join our patreon, all of
your support in views, comments, likes, and

00:13:15.649 --> 00:13:20.149
questions helps us figure out where to take
our journey next.

00:13:20.149 --> 00:13:27.220
So thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:33.759
And again thank you to our Patreon patrons,
all of these people here, who yes, as I have

00:13:33.759 --> 00:13:38.079
just said, are the reason that this show can
continue to exist.

00:13:38.079 --> 00:13:43.989
So, if you are thankful for this show, these
are the people you need to thank.

00:13:43.989 --> 00:13:48.709
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James Weiss, check out Jam &amp; Germs

00:13:48.709 --> 00:13:49.999
on Instagram.

00:13:49.999 --> 00:13:53.980
And if you want to see more from us, there’s
always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

